meta {
  name: Refresh - replay within the grace window
  type: http
  seq: 8
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "refresh_token": "{{spentRefreshToken}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("answers the replay instead of ending the session", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
    expect(res.body.access_token).to.be.a("string").and.not.be.empty;
  });

  test("keeps the client in the same family rather than opening a new session", function () {
    expect(res.body.refresh_token).to.not.equal(bru.getVar("spentRefreshToken"));
    expect(res.body.refresh_token).to.not.equal(bru.getVar("refreshToken"));
  });

  test("the successor issued before the replay still works", function () {
    expect(res.body.expires_in).to.equal(900);
  });
}

docs {
  `Auth::RefreshTokens::GRACE` — ten seconds during which a second presentation
  of an already-spent token is read as the same client retrying, not as somebody
  else holding a copy.

  It exists because "presented twice" has two causes and only one is theft. Two
  browser tabs whose access tokens expire in the same second send the same
  refresh token milliseconds apart; so does a reload racing a poll, or a retry
  after a response was lost coming back. Without the window every one of those
  revoked the family and signed the person out of every tab — a bug that fired
  on ordinary use and looked, from the server's side, exactly like the attack it
  was meant to catch.

  What it costs, stated rather than discovered: only the digest of a refresh
  token is stored, so the first successor's raw string cannot be handed back and
  a second one is issued instead. For up to ten seconds a family carries two
  live tokens.

  The theft half is the next request, which is also why this collection takes
  ten seconds longer than it otherwise would.
}
